// Pictures of what the code draws, for the slides whose code draws something
// this page cannot.
//
// Every other example on this tour is shown by being *run*: the interface
// around them is hive.ui, so a view in a slide is a view the page can render
// and does. Two slides are not like that, and it is worth saying exactly why
// rather than leaving a reader to wonder where the pictures went.
//
// `hive.ui.scene` draws in a window and only in a window. Its shapes never
// reach the document — they travel on a frame of their own, so a world can be
// redrawn sixty times a second without the page around it being re-sent — and
// the three.js that draws them is served by the window's own server, off
// routes this program has no way to register. Hand a scene to `ui.page` and
// what comes back is `<div class="h-scene"></div>`: the box, correctly sized,
// with nothing in it. That is not a gap in hive.ui. A scene is a thing you
// stand in front of and turn your head in, and a page that answers a request
// and then forgets you is the wrong shape for one.
//
// So the picture is taken rather than drawn. What is below is a real window,
// running the slide's own example, photographed: the program was built, run,
// and its window loaded in a browser that captured what three.js put on the
// canvas. Nothing was posed and nothing was drawn by hand — the shapes, the
// grass, the shadow and the black sky above it are what that code does, and
// the sky is black because the example asks for `fog` and never names a
// `background`.
//
// It is a palette PNG, which for a render of five solid colours on a lawn is
// a fifth of the bytes of the truecolour original and no visible difference:
// 1640 wide for a column that shows it at half that, so it stays sharp on a
// screen that draws two pixels for one.
//
// The decoding is ./icon's, because the convention is ./icon's: base64 folded
// at 76 columns, unfolded before Go's decoder ever sees a newline. One rule,
// written once, wherever a file in this program carries bytes as text.
import ./icon

// Where the picture lives, and what it is. The path is here rather than in
// main.hive for the same reason the bytes are: a slide names the picture it
// wants, and this is the file that knows which pictures there are.
func scenePath(): Str {
	return "/shot/three-dimensions.png"
}

func scene(): Result<Str, hive.crypto.CryptoError> {
	return icon.decode(sceneBase64())
}

